From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 19:42:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 079/184] segacd: consume ring entries just-decoded, and
 distrust slow transfers

Hardware named the recycler: the last wrong grab delivered wanted+5,
and five is the write head's wrap distance -- a slot is rewritten about
five blocks after insertion. Worse, Fsfirst -33 alongside passing
header checks means the rewrite can land between the header words
leaving the machine and the data behind them: right header, wrong
bytes, a directory that reads back plausible and empty.

Entries are now consumed at age one or fresher (the BIOS consumes at
zero to one; at one the rewrite is four sector times out and the
transfer needs half of one), and after every transfer the slot's total
age is checked -- reaching rewrite range disqualifies the bytes no
matter what the header said.

Also: EmuTOS cprintf has no '+' flag; the diagnostic line is compacted
to fit forty columns with the delta printed as plain %d.
---
 bios/bios.c   |  2 +-
 bios/segacd.c | 15 +++++++++++++--
 2 files changed, 14 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/bios.c b/bios/bios.c
index ba11692..736ffdf 100644
--- a/bios/bios.c
+++ b/bios/bios.c
@@ -1112,7 +1112,7 @@ void biosmain(void)
                             &bad, &wrong, &torn, &flag, &wdelta);
             cprintf(" drive %X  seek %u  wait %u  err %u  why %04X\r\n",
                     state, seeks, hits, errs, why);
-            cprintf(" torn %u  badbcd %u  wrong %u (last %+d)  flag %u\r\n\r\n",
+            cprintf(" torn %u bcd %u wr %u l%d fl %u\r\n\r\n",
                     torn, bad, wrong, wdelta, flag);
 
             /* Long enough to read a round before the next scrolls it
diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index 49fc8af..7dd8b5c 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -288,6 +288,8 @@ static volatile UBYTE cdc_ints;         /* sectors the decoder has reported;
                                          * declared here because the ring's
                                          * freshness is measured in it */
 
+static UBYTE cd_found_stamp;    /* insertion stamp of the entry returned */
+
 static BOOL cd_ring_find(ULONG lba, UWORD *pt)
 {
     UBYTE i, best = 0xFF, age;
@@ -313,9 +315,10 @@ static BOOL cd_ring_find(ULONG lba, UWORD *pt)
              * seven deep -- at age four plus two in flight the slot is
              * being rewritten mid-grab. The BIOS consumes its entries
              * at age zero or one; two is already generous. */
-            if (age <= 2 && age < best) {
+            if (age <= 1 && age < best) {
                 best = age;
                 *pt = cd_ring_pt[i];
+                cd_found_stamp = cd_ring_age[i];
                 found = TRUE;
             }
         }
@@ -1936,7 +1939,15 @@ static BOOL cd_fetch(ULONG lba)
         continue;
 
 collect:
-        if (cdc_grab(pt)) {
+        if (cdc_grab(pt)
+            /* The header check can be beaten: the recycler can rewrite
+             * the slot between the header words leaving the machine and
+             * the data behind them -- right header, wrong bytes, which
+             * is how a directory reads back plausible and empty. The
+             * defense is arithmetic: if the slot's total age by the end
+             * of the transfer reaches rewrite range, the bytes are not
+             * trusted no matter what the header said. */
+            && (UBYTE)(cdc_ints - cd_found_stamp) <= 4) {
             /* The step the recipe always had and this driver never
              * did: "compare the header against the frame expected,
              * else start over". The BIOS does it; megadev and Lunar
